where <math>\text{I}_x</math>, <math>\text{I}_y</math> are the principal moments of inertia of the water-plane. Hence
{{block center|<math>x = -\frac{\theta}{\text{V}}\cdot\text{I}_y\cdot\sin\phi;</math>}}
{{block center|<math>y = \frac{\theta}{\text{V}}\cdot\text{I}_x\cdot\cos\phi;</math>}}
{{block center|<math>z = \tfrac{1}{2}\frac{\theta^2}{\text{V}}(\text{I}_x\cos^2\phi + \text{I}_y\sin^2\phi).</math>}}
Eliminating <math>\theta</math> and <math>\phi</math>, the locus of the centre of buoyancy for small inclinations of the ship becomes the elliptic paraboloid—
{{block center|<math>2z = \frac{x^2}{\text{I}_y/\text{V}} + \frac{y^2}{\text{I}_x/\text{V}}.</math>}}
The equation to the indicatrix referred to axes parallel to <math>\text{B}x</math>, <math>\text{B}y</math> is therefore
{{block center|<math>\frac{x^2}{\text{I}_y/\text{V}} + \frac{y^2}{\text{I}_x/\text{V}} = \text{constant};</math>}}

readings.c1s2:
and the indicatrix is therefore similar and similarly situated to the momental ellipse of the water-plane, and the surface of buoyancy is everywhere synclastic and concave to all points within it. The quantities <math>\text{I}_y/\text{V}</math> and <math>\text{I}_x/\text{V}</math> are evidently equal to <math>\text{BM}_x</math> and <math>\text{BM}_y</math> (referring to inclinations about <math>\text{O}y</math> and <math>\text{O}x</math> respectively); and the indicatrix and momental ellipse become
{{block center|<math>\frac{x^2}{\text{BM}_y} + \frac{y^2}{\text{BM}_x} = \text{constant}.</math>}}
The angle <math>\psi</math> that <math>\text{BB}_2</math> (the projection of <math>\text{BB}'</math> on the plane of the indicatrix) makes with <math>x\text{O}</math> is given by
{{block center|<math>\tan\psi = -\frac{y}{x} = \frac{\text{I}_x}{\text{I}_y}.\cot\phi</math>;}}
hence the direction is conjugate to that of the axis of rotation with respect to the indicatrix.

readings.c1s4:
QN is the projection on the plane of the figure of the normal to the surface at <math>\text{B}^1</math>, and the shortest distance between the normals at B and <math>\text{B}^1</math> is equal to <math>\text{ON} = \text{MQ} = \text{B}_1\text{B}_2 = \frac{\text{P}\theta}{\text{V}}</math>, since ON or the axis of inclination is perpendicular to them both. Also, the length <math>\text{B}'\text{M}</math> of the normal at <math>\text{B}'</math> intercepted between <math>\text{B}''</math> and the foot of the common perpendicular is equal to <math>\frac{\text{QN}}{\theta}</math> since <math>\theta</math> is the angle between the normals at B and <math>\text{B}'</math>; it follows that
{{block center|<math>\text{B}'\text{M}' = \frac{\text{BB}_1}{\theta} = \frac{\text{I}_x'}{\text{V}}</math>,}}
an expression analogous to that obtained before for the case of small inclinations in the direction of the principal axes of the water-plane. It is worthy of note that the radius of curvature <math>\rho</math> of the normal section of the surface of buoyancy through <math>\text{O}y'</math> is, in general, less than BM; the latter being equal to <math>\frac{\text{OM}^2}{2z}</math>, and <math>\rho</math> being equal to <math>\frac{\text{OR}^2}{2z}</math>; <math>\rho</math> is also obtainable by Euler's equation—
{{block center|<math>\frac{1}{\rho} = \frac{\cos^2\phi}{\text{BM}_x} + \frac{\sin^2\phi}{\text{BM}_y}</math>,}}
becoming equal to BM for inclinations about the principal axes. Similarly the radius of curvature of the normal section through Q is, in general, greater than BM.

